---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro'

const title = 'About | Klaroly'
const description =
  'Klaroly is built by Sunday South, a UK studio, alongside a working wedding makeup artist. Here is why we are building it.'
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={description}>
  <div class="mx-auto max-w-4xl px-5 sm:px-8">
    <section class="pt-16 pb-2 sm:pt-24">
      <h1 class="text-4xl leading-[1.1] font-semibold tracking-tight sm:text-5xl">
        Why we are building this
      </h1>
      <p
        class="mt-6 max-w-xl text-lg leading-relaxed text-[var(--color-muted)] dark:text-[var(--color-muted-dark)]"
      >
        Klaroly started with a wedding makeup artist complaining about her admin.
      </p>
    </section>

    <div class="prose max-w-2xl">
      <p>
        Not about the work. The work is the good bit. About the twelve places a
        booking lives before it becomes a wedding: the Instagram message, the
        email, the note in her phone, the calendar entry, the contract template
        she keeps meaning to update, the invoice, the reminder she forgot to send.
      </p>
      <p>
        There is software for this. Most of it was built for salons, where the
        client comes to you and books a forty-minute slot. A wedding is a
        different shape entirely. It is booked eighteen months out, it involves
        five people, it happens somewhere you have to drive to at five in the
        morning, and the money arrives in two pieces months apart. Bending salon
        software around that is possible. It is just not much fun.
      </p>
      <p>So we are building something for the actual shape of the job.</p>

      <h2>Who is behind it</h2>
      <p>
        Klaroly is made by <strong>Sunday South</strong>, a small UK studio that has
        been building web and mobile products for other people for fifteen years.
        This one is ours.
      </p>
      <p>
        It is being designed alongside a working wedding makeup artist, who is the
        reason any of the details above are right, and tested with other artists
        who have kindly agreed to tell us when we have got something wrong.
      </p>

      <h2>How we are building it</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Slowly enough to get it right, and in public.</strong> We are talking
          to artists before writing the features, not after.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Small on purpose.</strong> We would rather do the booking, contract
          and invoice side properly than do nine things adequately. Things we have
          deliberately left out are left out, not forgotten.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Not funded by anybody.</strong> No investors, no growth targets, no
          pressure to add features nobody asked for. It is paid for by the people who
          use it, which keeps the incentives simple.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Where we are going</h2>
      <p>
        Wedding makeup first, then hair, then the other people who turn up at a
        wedding with a car full of kit. The problems are close enough to share a
        product and different enough to be worth doing carefully, one at a time.
      </p>
      <p><a href="/contact">Get in touch</a></p>
    </div>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
